<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/eugippius"><span class="enc-name">Eugippius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eugippius was a monk and disciple of Saint Severinus, who left Noricum with the Roman population in 488 and became abbot of a monastery near Naples.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/euin-of-trent"><span class="enc-name">Euin of Trent</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Euin was the first Lombard Duke of Trent, named by Paul the Deacon, ruling from around 569 or 575 until his death in 595.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eulalia-of-merida"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eulalia-of-merida.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eulalia of Mérida</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eulalia of Mérida was a young Christian martyred at Emerita Augusta, capital of Roman Lusitania, in 304 during the persecution of Diocletian.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eulji-mundeok"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eulji-mundeok.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eulji Mundeok</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eulji Mundeok was a Goguryeo general who led the defence against the invasion of Emperor Yang of Sui in 612.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/eulmash-shakin-shumi"><span class="enc-name">Eulmash-shakin-shumi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eulmash-shakin-shumi was king of Babylon from about 1004 to 988 BCE and founder of the short-lived Bazi Dynasty, which followed the Second Sealand Dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eulogius-of-cordoba"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eulogius-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eulogius of Córdoba</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eulogius of Córdoba was a Christian priest and writer in Umayyad al-Andalus and the chief chronicler and defender of the Martyrs of Córdoba.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/eumelus-of-bosporus"><span class="enc-name">Eumelus of Bosporus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eumelus was king of the Bosporan Kingdom from about 309 to 304 BCE, a son of Paerisades I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eumenes-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eumenes I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eumenes I was ruler of Pergamon from 263 to 241 BCE, the nephew and adopted heir of Philetaerus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eumenes-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eumenes II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eumenes II was king of Pergamon in the early second century BCE and an ally of Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-of-cardia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eumenes-of-cardia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eumenes of Cardia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eumenes of Cardia was a Greek general and scholar from Cardia in the Thracian Chersonese who served as royal secretary to Philip II and Alexander the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/eunones"><span class="enc-name">Eunones</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eunones was king of the Aorsi, a Sarmatian people north of the Caucasus, in the mid-1st century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/eunostos-of-soloi"><span class="enc-name">Eunostos of Soloi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eunostos was the last king of Soloi in Cyprus, ruling at the end of the 4th century BCE as an ally of Ptolemy I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eunuch"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eunuch.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eunuch</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A eunuch is a castrated man, and in many courts eunuchs served as palace officials, guards of the royal household and administrators.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eupalinos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eupalinos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eupalinos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eupalinos of Megara was a Greek engineer of the sixth century BCE who built the aqueduct tunnel on Samos for the tyrant Polycrates.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eupen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eupen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eupen</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Eupen is a town in the province of Liège in eastern Belgium, near the German border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/euphemius"><span class="enc-name">Euphemius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Euphemius was a Byzantine naval commander in Sicily who rebelled against the emperor Michael II in 826 and proclaimed himself emperor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/euphrates"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/imag